Hello Haitham Alqadamani ,

Where Am I? What=E2=80=99s in This Folder? Let=E2=80=99s Find Out.

First Lesson of Any Linux Journey: Know Where You Are

When you SSH into a Linux server or run a container shell, your very first =
question should be:

=E2=80=9CWhere am I in the filesystem?=E2=80=9D

The Linux filesystem is hierarchical - like a tree.

You always start at the root, /.

Everything - files, folders, config, users, devices - lives somewhere under=
 /.

Quick Start Commands

Command

Purpose

pwd

Show Present Working Directory

ls

List files in the current directory

cd /path/dir

Change directory

cd ~ or cd

Go to your home directory

cd -

Jump to your previous directory

=F0=9F=93=8C These commands are your daily toolkit.

Filesystem Tree

/

=E2=94=9C=E2=94=80=E2=94=80 bin/

=E2=94=9C=E2=94=80=E2=94=80 etc/

=E2=94=9C=E2=94=80=E2=94=80 home/

=E2=94=82   =E2=94=94=E2=94=80=E2=94=80 devops/

=E2=94=82       =E2=94=9C=E2=94=80=E2=94=80 scripts/

=E2=94=82       =E2=94=94=E2=94=80=E2=94=80 notes.txt

=E2=94=9C=E2=94=80=E2=94=80 var/

=E2=94=94=E2=94=80=E2=94=80 usr/

Your Location Changes:

$ pwd

/home/devops/scripts

You are inside the scripts folder of the devops user.

Important Directories You'll Visit Often

Directory

What's Inside

/

The root of all things

/home/

Personal folders for users

/etc/

Configuration files

/var/

Logs, spool data, variable app files

/usr/

User binaries and read-only system resources

/bin/

Essential command binaries (ls, cat, etc.)

You don=E2=80=99t have to memorize all paths. Just know where to look when =
debugging.

Try It Yourself (On Any Linux System)

pwd              # Where am I?

ls               # What's here?

cd /etc          # Jump to config folder

ls -l            # List with details

cd ~             # Back home

cd -             # Jump to last location

=F0=9F=93=8C These might be the first 6 commands you'll type when debugging=
 a Pod or EC2 instance.

Summary Table

Concept

Meaning

Filesystem Root

/ is the top of the Linux filesystem

pwd

Shows your current location

cd

Moves you around

ls

Lists files/folders

Key folders

/home, /etc, /var, /bin, /usr

Quick Review

- What does cd - do?
- Where would you find the nginx.conf file?
- Which directory holds logs like syslog?

Answers:

- Goes to previous directory
- Likely /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
- Logs live in /var/log/

